On 23.10.2012 22:50, derek.truesdale@anritsu.com wrote:
> On Tuesday, October 23, 2012 12:07:40 PM UTC-7, Janis wrote: [various
> comments on how missiles aren't a nuisance snipped]
> 
> I still think missiles take more time than melee.

With single-shot missiles I certainly agree, with multi-shot missiles I've
regularly observed even the opposite to be the case.

> 
> I'm not saying that the nuisance isn't minor.  especially if you name your
> missiles and put them in your autopickup exceptions.  but I still say that
> it's more time to type f-left than left.

UI typing ergonomy/speed is suboptimal in Nethack at certain places, I agree.
Though, the 'fire' command I don't see as a handicap; I often repeat commands
with just ^A.

>>> If I had a permanent source of Elbereth or levitation, my first wish
>>> would be for some ?oGenocide to get dragon scales.
>> 
>> If I had a wish I'd rather wish for a *guaranteed* dragon scale mail.
> 
> A single scroll of genocide gives you a 89% or so chance at scales.

Yes, I know, but 89% is significantly less than 100%. Mind the RNG! If I can
choose a certain gain that instantly grants high AC and MR (or alternatively
AC and reflection) and thus increases my survival rate at no cost from, say,
5% to 95%, I will do that. Shifting survivability (instead of 95%) to 96% in
89% of the cases, and leaving it at 5% in 11% of the cases I consider not to
be the right thing to do.

>>> If I didn't have a wand of fire to safely get scales,
>> Why is that safe? - The dragon will get scared and breath you to death 
>> from distance (especially pre-Castle when you're not prepared and where 
>> wishes are not yet guaranteed).
> 
> It's safe as long as you don't mistype your way off of your square. You can
> build a safe Elbereth cage without too much trouble.  The dragons can't
> move away from you so they can't hit you.

I see. But I am astonished that you take that huge effort where above you
said that even picking up missiles is a time burden to you.

> 
>> *when* I will do that; e.g. in some games I prefer getting the Int gain 
>> from their corpses, so some means of ranged attack (or permanent
>> Elbereth)
> 
> I almost always get Int gain from blessed gain ability.

If I have those potions I do as well gain Int from those. But first you need
to find some - they are not too common -, and the earlier you increase your
Int the earlier you can reliably read spellbooks and can cast spells to your
advantage and survival rate. So, depending on the class played, getting Int
mid-game from mind flayers is an option that I may or may not want to erase.

> 
>> is crucial. Sometimes, in the late game, I leave L's alive to easily
>> find the upstairs in Gehennom (while exploring bottum-up). An occasional
>> random athame is also a nice dropping.
> 
> yeah, the upstair detection is nice, I'll give you that.  The cursing is
> annoying, though.

Indeed. That's why a chargeless single-turn engraver, an athame, is so good!
Curses won't hit you when you stand on the E-word.

> 
>> Wishing for an athame, instead, solves problems with many more monsters.
> 
> In the early game an athame is extremely useful if you're not a tank.  With
> that start you should have been a tank. In the late game, wands of fire and
> lightning are so bountiful that they're polyfodder to me.

Such huge amounts of those two wand types is rarely true for my games. And I
value athames high until the end. They make even permanent engravings quite
redundant (or necessary only for heavy melee combat from a single spot; for
that latter case of crowds of monster a ring of conflict is as good, though).

> 
>> But I wonder about one thing; I thought that engraving with blessed
>> artifact weapons will not dull them.[*]
> 
> [...] I don't suppose that the time your
> artifact didn't dull your artifact happened to be Magicbane?

I am sure it was not Magicbane. (I wouldn't have enchanted Magicbane to +6.)

